# Target


**Application:** PortSwigger Web Security Academy Lab


**Affected Endpoint:**


```text
/admin
Objective

Bypass the access control protecting the administrative interface and delete the user carlos.

Methodology
Attempt to access the administrative interface directly.
Observe that direct access to /admin is blocked.
Intercept the request using Burp Suite.
Test whether the application trusts alternative URL-related HTTP headers.
Identify that X-Original-URL controls backend routing.
Use the header to access /admin.
Identify the administrative delete functionality.
Use X-Original-URL to access the delete endpoint.
Delete the user carlos.
Reconnaissance

The first step was attempting to access the administrative interface directly:

GET /admin HTTP/2
Host: LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net

The application denied access to the resource.

This indicated that the administrative functionality was protected by an access control mechanism.

Testing X-Original-URL

The request was sent to Burp Repeater for further testing.

The request URI was changed to / and an X-Original-URL header was added:

GET / HTTP/2
Host: LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net
X-Original-URL: /invalid

The server returned a response corresponding to the /invalid path.

This indicated that the backend was processing the value supplied through the X-Original-URL header.

Access Control Bypass

The X-Original-URL value was changed from /invalid to /admin:

GET / HTTP/2
Host: LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net
X-Original-URL: /admin

The application returned the administrative interface.

This confirmed that the access control could be bypassed.

The front-end component effectively saw:

/

while the backend interpreted the request as:

/admin
Exploitation

After accessing the administrative interface, the available administrative functionality was inspected.

The panel contained functionality for deleting users.

The relevant endpoint was:

/admin/delete

The target user was:

carlos

The request was therefore constructed as:

GET /?username=carlos HTTP/2
Host: LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net
X-Original-URL: /admin/delete

The backend interpreted the request as:

/admin/delete?username=carlos

The deletion operation was executed successfully.

