Technical Analysis

The vulnerability was caused by inconsistent URL interpretation between the front-end component and the backend application.

The request could be represented as:

Attacker
   │
   │ GET /
   │ X-Original-URL: /admin
   ▼
Reverse Proxy
   │
   │ Checks "/"
   │
   │ Request allowed
   ▼
Backend Application
   │
   │ Processes X-Original-URL
   ▼
/admin
   │
   ▼
Administrative Interface

The important difference was:

URL checked by the front-end:


/

versus:

URL processed by the backend:


/admin

Because the access-control mechanism evaluated a different URL from the one ultimately processed by the application, the restriction could be bypassed.

Root Cause

The root cause was trusting a client-controlled HTTP header to determine the resource being accessed.

The application trusted:

X-Original-URL

while the access-control mechanism evaluated the URL in the request line.

This created a discrepancy between authorization and request processing.

An attacker could therefore send:

GET /
X-Original-URL: /admin

instead of:

GET /admin

The first request bypassed the front-end restriction while still reaching the privileged backend resource.

Impact

An attacker could potentially:

Bypass access-control restrictions.
Access administrative functionality.
Execute privileged operations.
Delete users.
Modify sensitive application data.
Access other protected endpoints.

The final impact depends on the functionality exposed through the protected administrative interface.

Security Recommendations
Do Not Trust Client-Controlled Routing Headers

Headers such as:

X-Original-URL
X-Rewrite-URL
X-Forwarded-Prefix

should not be trusted when supplied directly by an external client.

Strip Internal Headers

If these headers are required for internal routing, the reverse proxy should remove externally supplied versions and generate trusted values itself.

For example:

proxy_set_header X-Original-URL "";
proxy_set_header X-Rewrite-URL "";
Perform Authorization on the Final Resource

Authorization must be performed against the actual resource that will be processed by the backend.

The application should not authorize:

/

and subsequently execute:

/admin

without performing authorization against /admin.

Maintain Consistent URL Interpretation

All components involved in processing a request should agree on the effective URL.

A secure request flow should be:

HTTP Request
     │
     ▼
URL Normalization
     │
     ▼
URL Rewriting
     │
     ▼
Authorization
     │
     ▼
Resource Execution
Enforce Server-Side Authorization

Administrative privileges should always be validated server-side based on trusted authentication and authorization information.

Access to endpoints such as:

/admin
/admin/delete

should require an appropriate authenticated role regardless of how the request URL was constructed.

